#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c0350c-0471-55de-ac70-f2529da87d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525251} {:answer/id #uuid "1bed26d7-5712-5fad-9a47-4f08bb3eb187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525265} {:answer/id #uuid "33b6e47c-35b6-5317-94f8-1bf5ec2d2141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525335} {:answer/id #uuid "544af06f-53bf-5a45-9d45-b81c4c92cd56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525425} {:answer/id #uuid "9042a2f9-0756-5b9d-ad5e-9f3310959eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525251} :answer-field/id #uuid "04e2e485-a811-5a11-9f6d-621be11e5c98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525849}] :question/id #uuid "1b0e0b9e-9780-58a6-9804-62e273336ea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-345598" :question/problem "A water pollution researcher measured $4$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ for each water sample in a region with suspicions of water pollution. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular sample.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=9,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe researcher then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $4\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(9)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  researcher used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(9)^{2} \\\\  & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{81}{95} \\\\  & ≈0.853 \\\\  & =85.3\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01d7962d-301d-5564-b84c-5c4c275c4f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525214} {:answer/id #uuid "2101ed94-0565-58d9-96d4-55fa409f7603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525280} {:answer/id #uuid "62e6b56c-6deb-528d-b4f2-3731cf383635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525455} {:answer/id #uuid "d844e003-3bf2-5bc1-b18b-41cf1342c36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96.0%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525743} {:answer/id #uuid "f856d479-2998-5675-8c7e-c2b51e51ddc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525280} :answer-field/id #uuid "2547a1a4-4d8c-5e2b-a028-871e88aaff4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525862}] :question/id #uuid "544f3b17-0710-5a62-83f3-a61ed0f7bcdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-345600" :question/problem "A quality inspector measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each machine in a factory. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular machine.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=4,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.5.\n$$\n\nThe inspector then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =57.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the inspector used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(4)^{2} \\\\  & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{52}{57.25} \\\\  & ≈0.908 \\\\  & =90.8\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a97efd-6507-568d-a733-df35803cdd38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525279} {:answer/id #uuid "37741afd-0543-580c-8a1e-3d6da126b644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525342} {:answer/id #uuid "b05f07be-33d9-5fa6-b04b-06111535bbdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525653} {:answer/id #uuid "da8a6c89-592b-5d39-adbd-d1d8791272ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525747} {:answer/id #uuid "dab33267-108b-5e93-b7c9-075b6c4a7248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525747} :answer-field/id #uuid "337c107f-d7b5-5336-9dff-a1373b9e6812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525873}] :question/id #uuid "7dcc2367-ae8e-5075-82b4-bcd5d8410a2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-345605" :question/problem "A nutritionist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each client in a group under a specific diet. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular client.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.6.\n$$\n\nThe nutritionist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.6)^{2} \\\\  & =50.36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the nutritionist used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2} \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{45}{50.36} \\\\  & ≈0.894 \\\\  & =89.4\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516d873e-ccd4-5077-a4ce-d8e999a1ad90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525417} {:answer/id #uuid "7373c834-8f8c-55c5-9e2e-b8721d241460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525499} {:answer/id #uuid "8d89f02b-374b-52f6-a7e9-61fe7c34455e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525559} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc471c5-7d2f-5671-88e6-6184f6fbad95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525568} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4774cd-feeb-566a-8ec8-b2c92c225f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525499} :answer-field/id #uuid "266486de-9240-55f9-95f5-d5273efe84c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525864}] :question/id #uuid "e91cdcf4-5450-5f10-aac1-2b321d212e78" :question/math-academy-id "q-345607" :question/problem "A pharmacologist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each drug among a group of potential treatments for high blood pressure. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular drug.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.5,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=1.\n$$\n\nThe pharmacologist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $5\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1.5)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =52.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the researcher used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2} \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{36}{52.25} \\\\  & ≈0.689 \\\\  & =68.9\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa5625e-af4e-5cb0-be77-41320df40a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528373} {:answer/id #uuid "29775c65-a127-562d-905b-ce5a0a1506c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528403} {:answer/id #uuid "bd240f70-4ee4-553a-b8a1-f11f13074026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528760} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c6296bc-3942-5538-b3c5-b580c49e804f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528946} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3fa802e-1a7c-5131-9274-0f3fd251222c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528697} {:answer/id #uuid "abe5576c-3f57-582e-a2f6-b681ef2c6ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528717} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0f7340-726e-57e2-8594-d7697b1e2ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528754} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa5d643-96f3-5f51-b2d9-8f6ea8ab456c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528754} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dfae55b-dcf9-5c8f-99dd-37e10549a26b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529027}] :question/id #uuid "3158298f-c612-536d-8b25-00f57fa2f77e" :question/math-academy-id "q-355844" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.74 & 0.61 & 0.89 \\\\ 0.38 & 0 & 0.61 \\\\ -0.92 & -0.03 & -0.87 \\\\ -1.2 & -1.61 & -1.28 \\\\ 1.01 & 1.02 & 0.65\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.389 & 0.167 & -0.312 & 0.84 & -0.133 \\\\ -0.174 & 0.453 & -0.384 & -0.192 & 0.762 \\\\ 0.321 & -0.749 & -0.348 & 0.232 & 0.402 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.384 & 0.359 & 0.419 & 0.22 \\\\ -0.464 & -0.242 & 0.712 & 0.166 & 0.438\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.329 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.311 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.591 & 0.31 & 0.745 \\\\ -0.558 & -0.823 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.582 & 0.475 & -0.66\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $95\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.329,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=0.9,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.311.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.329}^{2}}{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}+{0.311}^{2}}≈92.4\\%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}}{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}+{0.311}^{2}}≈99.2\\%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $92.4\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $99.2\\%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/e2/e2af29d0509f7ae3e3826cd35d3190b478e9c2415c00fc0e3798800d33381c59.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $95\\%$ of the variance."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e00e59c-c2e1-5d95-88f6-75a0287bc55a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528417} {:answer/id #uuid "359ded72-a1d6-5847-8db5-fe5d7574386d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528437} {:answer/id #uuid "64e8fa21-4152-565d-bf5a-4add5225dbab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528540} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd94c47-0c6d-5b9d-a0a0-01f30dc5aa0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528540} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c994f1c-25ce-53c1-b2bc-ad51547e017a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528999} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b9077d-bc80-505b-8b3d-48730306347d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528347} {:answer/id #uuid "162330ce-ca49-5e79-89b6-d89a13587017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528355} {:answer/id #uuid "beb61f16-161b-5af3-980b-30635c4dc9c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528765} {:answer/id #uuid "c1060128-a39b-5154-8642-ecf7080795dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528772} :answer-field/id #uuid "8575b8f8-052d-504c-a3e1-9051091e1d1f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529014}] :question/id #uuid "439142f8-6932-5f54-8d84-ca7931d891df" :question/math-academy-id "q-355908" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.36 & 1.7 & 0.25 & 1.05 \\\\ 0.8 & -0.33 & -1.37 & -0.53 \\\\ -0.51 & -0.23 & 0.05 & -0.61 \\\\ -1.18 & -0.96 & -0.32 & -1.01 \\\\ 1.25 & -0.18 & 1.4 & 1.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.477 & 0.704 & -0.239 & 0.135 & 0.448 \\\\ -0.291 & -0.357 & -0.764 & 0.049 & 0.449 \\\\ -0.217 & 0.106 & 0.24 & -0.83 & 0.442 \\\\ -0.55 & 0.135 & 0.445 & 0.529 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.582 & -0.589 & 0.323 & 0.104 & 0.447\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.041 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.967 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.671 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.268 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.356 & -0.757 & -0.46 & -0.298 \\\\ 0.454 & 0.644 & -0.416 & -0.454 \\\\ 0.493 & -0.1 & 0.783 & -0.366 \\\\ 0.652 & 0.041 & -0.052 & 0.755\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.041,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.967,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.671,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.268.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈57.9\\%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈82.1\\%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈99.6\\%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $57.9\\%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $82.1\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $99.6\\%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/5e/5eb43d1adcfa48f0fd66cd73ea05b40a9e937399457ba8614f380502c1c632dc.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc905bf-a333-5f88-bc94-7532cf83b587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528419} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6ed0a8-2992-50b5-a253-5ba14a0a4402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528597} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe3ce25-405b-53e4-8190-780d13dc0791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528683} {:answer/id #uuid "c7676032-6f77-5131-a597-0e7248c6766a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528785} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf9d5ef7-04cb-5f23-a601-17a6256765aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529040} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "270f63c7-1aeb-558c-8a65-03c3f71ad498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528400} {:answer/id #uuid "3678144c-c5c5-59eb-9c73-7f7cfc99f4a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528440} {:answer/id #uuid "8334f0e6-38a4-5448-b6ae-13c404a2e8fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528613} {:answer/id #uuid "bb438be0-e790-540f-8d81-6dbdb5c89782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528613} :answer-field/id #uuid "d87761c7-3333-5f96-b1b1-7878fa8dd615" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529063}] :question/id #uuid "9ccff77b-7d01-5bd7-aaf4-9fba844066ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-355903" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.46 & -0.2 & 0.07 & -0.88 \\\\ -1.4 & 0.15 & 0.73 & -0.87 \\\\ -0.24 & 1.53 & 0.68 & 1.35 \\\\ -0.15 & -1.25 & 0.23 & 0.73 \\\\ 1.32 & -0.23 & -1.72 & -0.32\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.186 & 0.257 & 0.252 & -0.792 & -0.456 \\\\ 0.398 & 0.593 & 0.3 & 0.45 & -0.443 \\\\ 0.529 & -0.708 & 0.123 & -0.057 & -0.447 \\\\ -0.015 & 0.115 & -0.887 & 0.015 & -0.446 \\\\ -0.725 & -0.259 & 0.208 & 0.408 & -0.443\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.878 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.152 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.636 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.652 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & -0.419 & 0.045 & -0.68 \\\\ 0.38 & -0.525 & 0.761 & 0.04 \\\\ 0.654 & 0.205 & -0.148 & -0.713 \\\\ 0.262 & -0.712 & -0.63 & 0.166\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.878,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2.152,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.636,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.652.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈51.7\\%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈80.6\\%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈97.3\\%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $51.7\\%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $80.6\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $97.3\\%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/cc/ccc57a7e978a8b123dd21da151c2b0fec3080be9d39f1c8f26cc3b19c0cbd2b1.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "780b7db4-aa11-5ca7-beff-a5f093f3ef15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528579} {:answer/id #uuid "8b461c20-1050-5d9a-91cd-8bc9ef709386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528633} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fe6545-9475-5823-b8b7-1c317fcf1b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528752} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6031ed-ea6d-5e9d-a2e7-a3897dbc6f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528803} :answer-field/id #uuid "0104538f-abdf-5164-878b-46a18f047fce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528937} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2646603c-69ca-578d-954e-dddbf2e2cb50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528399} {:answer/id #uuid "32fc720c-133a-5c75-a08a-0c1b2ac177b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528425} {:answer/id #uuid "e85b414d-9378-5c62-830c-20363052d009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528425} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4176f7b-81bc-51fd-bd74-a22248865cb9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529033}] :question/id #uuid "c6804c35-f8e1-5715-b170-24ee05ad3186" :question/math-academy-id "q-355842" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.42 & -0.33 & -0.4 \\\\ -0.94 & 1.07 & -0.08 \\\\ 1.11 & 0.12 & 1.71 \\\\ -0.79 & -1.51 & -0.9 \\\\ 1.04 & 0.66 & -0.32\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.244 & 0.013 & 0.022 & -0.603 & 0.759 \\\\ -0.051 & -0.763 & 0.397 & 0.405 & 0.307 \\\\ 0.671 & 0.439 & 0.394 & 0.235 & 0.383 \\\\ -0.644 & 0.452 & 0.021 & 0.57 & 0.237 \\\\ 0.272 & -0.144 & -0.828 & 0.305 & 0.355\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.730 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.751 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.210 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.617 & 0.395 & -0.68 \\\\ 0.461 & -0.882 & -0.095 \\\\ 0.638 & 0.255 & 0.727\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $85\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.730,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.751,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.210.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.730}^{2}}{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}+{1.210}^{2}}≈62.2\\%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}}{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}+{1.210}^{2}}≈87.8\\%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $62.2\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $87.8\\%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/b2/b2f5cc8cae6fac8d24e20c045990d39d088a34df26402a7f1e5137070579de03.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $85\\%$ of the variance."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f522d33-447f-5a33-87f8-afec283b06d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the direction in feature space" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528524} {:answer/id #uuid "e21e9b71-d5fe-5002-bbf9-c83a592a7e5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the datapoint" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the direction in feature space" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528524} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e39ffb1-f164-5926-be82-036b34a8c594" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528955} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "382eef9d-426a-51e6-9e4c-911bfc44034c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528443} {:answer/id #uuid "8666d778-17c2-5805-bc09-b0e72ffaab9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528620} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8b68a8-3248-5f95-9ab2-bff48b4d194e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528443} :answer-field/id #uuid "23a3adc0-daa9-5a27-927c-6db6359a3b42" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528959} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67fe181b-510e-519f-9c46-a25e8652a191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58.0%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528548} {:answer/id #uuid "793e5969-919c-5784-b4b3-609b44a5a9e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528584} {:answer/id #uuid "9873ac52-e07d-592a-b674-6f5be3695062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528662} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3a7eb2-6ace-5153-b421-4da362b6547d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.0%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528584} :answer-field/id #uuid "5986ba65-5099-53e3-88f5-f5e4fd954080" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528995} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b14cb21-f592-50e6-89dd-a0523693c954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/va