{:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542896} {:answer/id #uuid "83ecc3e9-7d0d-5aa5-9d43-3c35b8171289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "parallel" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542896} :answer-field/id #uuid "be6f8912-b155-50dc-a5eb-1a34f2982bfd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91625468-aa6c-5928-b5ee-247e48c0347c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543020} {:answer/id #uuid "9805513a-6601-50ed-ad0e-1d3229a7b1b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543036} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d47748-b2d4-53de-b3f6-1b101c2b5da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543020} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf80bec0-685f-5049-8ac7-2700161ad366" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dd04b3e-b93e-5143-94e2-77b20082200e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "principal component" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542978} {:answer/id #uuid "a3445924-2e9b-59a0-9f98-e399ab7e54a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "datapoint" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "principal component" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542978} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5533b86-b75f-5b4f-b691-ab7895f8ca87" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543446} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3832ca39-f3c3-5cf7-a6c7-58a65b261635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "standardize the data" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542787} {:answer/id #uuid "77c0b1c7-5ce3-5cd7-a8f0-146c0063bc0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center the data" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542956} {:answer/id #uuid "f7457e13-5bc3-5cb6-ae07-3ffe4c679ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "find the SVD of the data matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "standardize the data" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542787} :answer-field/id #uuid "de5e06b2-261b-5cb7-abf6-2181d50769ea" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543447}] :question/id #uuid "d96fd40c-87e8-5ffa-bd33-b4b724c33445" :question/math-academy-id "q-355958" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.795 & -5.044 & 1.480 \\\\ 2.471 & -4.893 & 1.132 \\\\ 2.589 & -6.272 & -0.161 \\\\ 0.548 & -6.608 & 1.020 \\\\ 5.379 & -3.807 & 3.126\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning that walks through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is \n{{field-1}} \nwith the \n{{field-2}} \nvariance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction in feature space \n{{field-3}} \nto the first\n{{field-4}}. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to {{field-5}}.\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=[\\begin{aligned}-0.81 & 0.25 & 0.14 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.38 & -0.16 \\\\ 0.12 & -0.84 & -1.25 \\\\ -0.94 & -1.13 & -0.25 \\\\ 1.57 & 1.34 & 1.53\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.066 & 0.590 & -0.424 & -0.067 & 0.680 \\\\ 0.055 & -0.056 & -0.505 & 0.841 & -0.178 \\\\ -0.374 & -0.731 & -0.063 & 0.057 & 0.565 \\\\ -0.430 & 0.317 & 0.675 & 0.487 & 0.152 \\\\ 0.817 & -0.116 & 0.325 & 0.220 & 0.405\\end{aligned}], \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.124 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.309 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.738 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.544 & -0.802 & 0.248 \\\\ 0.608 & 0.173 & -0.775 \\\\ 0.578 & 0.572 & 0.581\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n{{field-6}}.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n{{field-7}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-8}}.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={{field-9}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is $\\text{the direction in feature space}$ with the $\\text{most}$ variance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction $\\text{orthogonal}$ to the first $\\text{principal component}.$\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to $\\text{standardize the data}.$\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=[\\begin{aligned}-0.81 & 0.25 & 0.14 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.38 & -0.16 \\\\ 0.12 & -0.84 & -1.25 \\\\ -0.94 & -1.13 & -0.25 \\\\ 1.57 & 1.34 & 1.53\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.066 & 0.590 & -0.424 & -0.067 & 0.680 \\\\ 0.055 & -0.056 & -0.505 & 0.841 & -0.178 \\\\ -0.374 & -0.731 & -0.063 & 0.057 & 0.565 \\\\ -0.430 & 0.317 & 0.675 & 0.487 & 0.152 \\\\ 0.817 & -0.116 & 0.325 & 0.220 & 0.405\\end{aligned}], \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.124 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.309 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.738 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.544 & -0.802 & 0.248 \\\\ 0.608 & 0.173 & -0.775 \\\\ 0.578 & 0.572 & 0.581\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{3.124}^{2}}{{3.124}^{2}+{1.309}^{2}+{0.738}^{2}}≈81.2\\%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{3.124}^{2}+{1.309}^{2}}{{3.124}^{2}+{1.309}^{2}+{0.738}^{2}}≈95.5\\%.\n$$\n\nThus, we have that \n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $81.2\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $95.5\\%$ of the variance.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions}$ , since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={X}_{\\text{std}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nsince we need to project the data onto the first two principal components, which are the first two columns of $V.$\n\n**Note:** In order to invert the projection and interpret any further analysis in terms of the original features, we need to know the means and standard deviations of the original dataset $X.$ Additionally, in case we have more data, we can use the means and standard deviations of the original dataset to map the new data into the original feature space."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0231280f-3c96-52d4-bb02-f31de91a72d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$44.7$ inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550376} {:answer/id #uuid "2e066363-c73f-5f32-81f2-579f95f3d955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45.8$  inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550482} {:answer/id #uuid "54b64262-45e4-5112-8f5a-007f27ab1984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$46.9$ inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550568} {:answer/id #uuid "d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45.1$  inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550953} {:answer/id #uuid "e78f56b5-9629-515c-a62c-674b58e812ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$46.2$  inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45.1$  inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550953} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551139}] :question/id #uuid "1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad" :question/math-academy-id "q-41739" :question/problem "A physics experiment consists of a small object moving along a straight line. The following table contains data collected on the distance traveled by the object (in inches) during the first five seconds. \n\n| Time $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Distance $(y)$ | $8.6$ | $13.1$ | $24.6$ | $28.1$ | $38.6$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what distance will the object cover after $6$ seconds, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8.6 \\\\ 13.1 \\\\ 24.6 \\\\ 28.1 \\\\ 38.6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 7.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.1+7.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the distance the object will cover after $6$ seconds, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.1+7.5(6) \\\\  & =45.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the object will cover a distance of $45.1$ inches in $6$ seconds.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222d1b8c-2c04-568e-8da7-51e6ea7c8663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">From:</td> \n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Countryside</b></td> \n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">To:</td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.28$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.72$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.88$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550444} {:answer/id #uuid "6971dc04-f2d9-5e4d-af65-09fc1da42a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">From:</td> \n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Countryside</b></td> \n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">To:</td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.12$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.28$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.88$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.72$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550628} {:answer/id #uuid "8e327ddb-636a-51fe-9f9f-8dc1e67320c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">From:</td> \n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Countryside</b></td> \n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">To:</td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.28$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.88$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.72$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.12$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550729} {:answer/id #uuid "ae851e7a-57eb-52b1-8b52-dcf69a01f009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">From:</td> \n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Countryside</b></td> \n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">To:</td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.88$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.28$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.12$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.72$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550842} {:answer/id #uuid "f8546886-0820-5923-90be-94d7c0870ccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">From:</td> \n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Countryside</b></td> \n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">To:</td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.88$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.72$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.12$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.28$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">From:</td> \n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Countryside</b></td> \n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">To:</td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.88$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.28$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.12$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.72$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550842} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f6518b4-e34a-514d-90e9-c9eb50aaae11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551128}] :question/id #uuid "1cf7f6b8-f438-52c0-a338-61f2ad7fa03e" :question/math-academy-id "q-135555" :question/problem "In a particular country, there are two groups of people: those who live in the cities and those who live in the countryside. A study has shown that each decade \n\n- $12\\%$ of people who lived in the cities relocate to the countryside, and\n\n- $28\\%$ of people who lived in the countryside relocate to the cities.\n\nWhich of the following tables describes how these people change their preferences each year?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each decade: \n\n- $12\\%$ of people who live in the cities relocate to the countryside. This implies that $88\\%$ of them remain living in the cities.\n\n- $28\\%$ of people who live in the countryside relocate to the cities. This implies that $72\\%$ of them remain living in the countryside.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.88$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.28$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.12$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.72$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.88 & 0.28 \\\\ 0.12 & 0.72\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41265bdd-d701-5915-85ea-b8b48d3032b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550520} {:answer/id #uuid "702d8457-7a04-5f81-b354-457a65777dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550650} {:answer/id #uuid "9ebfa448-95fd-50cb-8944-07f3949c13df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550774} {:answer/id #uuid "a181d0c5-2f8c-57a8-860d-c4b8dd85032a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 19 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 14 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ -9 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ -11 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550784} {:answer/id #uuid "bd434e23-296a-58e6-8c06-72a49aab1760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 19 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 14 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ -9 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ -11 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550774} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c23c67-d5f0-5435-9ff0-f58fa6b0cfc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551214}] :question/id #uuid "1dfba625-81c0-581d-a463-4b6d422c9f73" :question/math-academy-id "q-139660" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $19$ | $14$ | $-9$ | $-11$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $19$ | $14$ | $-9$ | $-11$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105e81b4-ebb0-52a8-aaaf-535d61ac1ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550409} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc67c9e-b654-57e4-a474-667c5d454cc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550514} {:answer/id #uuid "40c82cec-2aab-5e87-a7f3-187df9be1327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550517} {:answer/id #uuid "76ffc93a-e662-59db-8132-6716efe1d93a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550668} {:answer/id #uuid "9af91aff-676a-57a8-ab5a-633b6dcd682f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550517} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2b5c355-cad0-5348-8dff-54a6b032b4f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551184}] :question/id #uuid "34952883-ed67-58e1-83a4-6ee369a6dde8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35847" :question/problem "A certain company produces $2$ types of flour, flour $A$ and flour $B.$ The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of flour each month:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Flour $A$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Flour $B$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Flour $A$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Flour $B$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThat is, $70\\%$ of flour $A$ customers switch to flour $B$ each month, and $60\\%$ of flour $B$ customers change to flour $A.$\n\nCurrently, $6\\,000$ customers use flour $A,$ while $4\\,000$ customers use flour $B.$ How many customers will use flour $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of flour is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6\\,000 \\\\ 4\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(10\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{10\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}6\\,000 \\\\ 4\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.42 \\\\ 0.58\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $42\\%$ of customers will use flour $A.$ Computing $42\\%$ of $10\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot 0.42=4\\,200.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\,200$ customers will use flour $A$ in one month."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02cb7704-5d65-5a8a-b6f4-5c1f2eca8d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550379} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb23ae6-3d85-59f7-9aa5-38c1889a0c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550476} {:answer/id #uuid "767ed063-1f3b-5581-a169-3766dae98d5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550666} {:answer/id #uuid "90a442b4-496e-5aff-8488-442c3527f982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550735} {:answer/id #uuid "ee44493c-7944-5d36-ba66-2bc4ba331705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550476} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4192aa-858b-5977-ae07-cf74a154f636" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551111}] :question/id #uuid "776ef62d-2c09-5e2b-a7f2-e7cbc949af5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-139644" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-8$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=-3-6x.$"}]}
